// WHY: A function parameter shadowed by an inner `let` inside an `if` body that
// contains a `wait`. The inner `let p = 99` is a DISTINCT binding but shares the
// name of the parameter `p`. `collect_crossings_in_stmts` filtered it out via
// `!param_names.contains(name)` — the inner binding was never added to the crossing
// set, so no sm_entry alloca was pre-created. `lower_stmt` then emitted a fresh alloca
// in the if-body block (not sm_entry), and `reload_params_from_frame` in the
// continuation state read cg.locals["p"] — now pointing to that non-entry alloca —
// producing an LLVM dominance ICE ("Instruction does not dominate all uses!").
//
// The fix (M3a locked decision, design/concurrency.md § ShadowsCrossingLocal):
// reject any nested `let` shadowing a parameter in an SM function, unconditionally.
// The `param_is_genuine_crossing_after_wait` gate was too narrow — the ICE occurs
// regardless of whether the parameter is read after the wait.
//
// This fixture MUST produce a clean compile error (exit 1) with the shadow
// diagnostic. It must NOT produce any ICE text ("does not dominate",
// "Machine-code generation failed", "compiler bug").
function worker(p: int) -> nothing {
  if (true) {
    let p: int = 99
    wait sleep(5)
    print(p.toString())
  }
}
function entrypoint() -> nothing {
  worker(3)
}
